var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"integration/#KiteModels-integration","page":"KiteModels integration","text":"A version of the autopilot.jl script from KiteControllers that uses this package is available as examples/autopilot_im_winch_FOC_F1.jl.\n\nThe patch examples/autopilot_im_winch_patch.jl is not runnable on its own — it is the minimal patch to apply to a fresh copy of the upstream autopilot example.","title":"KiteModels integration"},{"category":"section","location":"integration/#Two-integration-paths","page":"KiteModels integration","text":"There are two ways into KiteModels, and they are not equivalent.","title":"Two integration paths"},{"category":"section","location":"integration/#1.-step_drive_from_kite!-—-preferred","page":"KiteModels integration","text":"Update the electric drive exactly once per KiteControllers macro-step:\n\nTe_cmd = ElectricMachineWinch.step_drive_from_kite!(\n    app.kps4.wm,\n    v_ro_set,\n    v_ro_meas,\n    F_tether;\n    dt_outer = app.dt,\n)\n\nThen pass the resulting torque to the mechanical update:\n\nKiteModels.next_step!(\n    app.kps4,\n    integrator;\n    set_torque = Te_cmd,\n    dt = app.dt,\n)\n\nstep_drive_from_kite! internally executes round(Int, dt_outer/wm.Ts) electrical/controller substeps to cover the KiteControllers macro-step, holding the kite-side variables constant during that macro-step.\n\nChoose dt_outer so that dt_outer / wm.Ts is an integer, or extremely close to one. Otherwise the effective electrical sample time drifts away from the Ts baked into the observer and controller parameter objects.","title":"1. step_drive_from_kite! — preferred"},{"category":"section","location":"integration/#2.-calc_acceleration(...;-set_speed-...)-—-convenient,-but-be-careful","page":"KiteModels integration","text":"The direct WinchModels.calc_acceleration interface advances the drive inside the call. It is convenient, it is what the tests use, and it remains available for simple integration tests and compatibility.\n\nIt is not the right path for the detailed discrete controllers, because a DAE solver may evaluate the mechanical residual or acceleration function more than once per macro-step. Advancing discrete observer/controller states inside every residual evaluation would produce a nonphysical number of controller updates. wm.n_acceleration_calls counts the calls, which makes this easy to detect.\n\ncalc_acceleration with set_torque — rather than set_speed — is the read-only companion to step_drive_from_kite!: it applies the given torque and returns the resulting reel-out acceleration without stepping the controller.","title":"2. calc_acceleration(...; set_speed = ...) — convenient, but be careful"},{"category":"section","location":"integration/#Adding-the-winch-to-a-KiteControllers-project","page":"KiteModels integration","text":"In your KiteControllers.jl project, add the bridge package to the environment:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.activate(\".\")\nPkg.develop(path=\"../ElectricMachineWinch.jl\")\nPkg.instantiate()\n\nThen copy the original autopilot example:\n\nexamples/autopilot.jl -> examples/autopilot_im_winch.jl\n\nand add near the top:\n\nusing ElectricMachineWinch\n\nImmediately after app.kps4 = KPS4(app.kcu), insert the winch. Start with the simple ideal-torque controller:\n\napp.kps4.wm = make_electric_winch(\n    controller = :ideal,\n    drum_radius = 0.5,\n    gear_ratio = 10.0,\n    J_eq = 0.3685,\n    B_eq = 0.01298,\n    Ts = app.dt,\n    Te_max = 124.0,\n)\n\nOnly after that works, switch to the FOC model:\n\napp.kps4.wm = make_electric_winch(\n    controller = :foc_speed_f1,\n    drum_radius = 0.2,\n    gear_ratio = 4.26,\n    J_eq = 0.204,\n    B_eq = 0.0804,\n    Ts = 1e-4,\n    plant_substeps = 1,\n    Vs_max = 310.0,\n    Is_max = 40.0 * sqrt(2),\n    Te_max = 124.0,\n    speed_ts_wm = 1.0,\n    use_load_feedforward = true,\n    use_field_weakening = true,\n    wm_base_fw = 120.0,\n)\n\nand switch the stepping over to step_drive_from_kite! as shown above.","title":"Adding the winch to a KiteControllers project"},{"category":"section","location":"api/winch/#Winch-model-and-KiteModels-interface","page":"Winch and interface","text":"","title":"Winch model and KiteModels interface"},{"category":"section","location":"api/winch/#Module","page":"Winch and interface","text":"","title":"Module"},{"category":"section","location":"api/winch/#Core-types","page":"Winch and interface","text":"","title":"Core types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/winch/#KiteModels-interface","page":"Winch and interface","text":"","title":"KiteModels interface"},{"category":"section","location":"api/winch/#Construction","page":"Winch and interface","text":"","title":"Construction"},{"category":"module","location":"api/winch/#ElectricMachineWinch.ElectricMachineWinch","page":"Winch and interface","text":"ElectricMachineWinch\n\nBridge package that exposes a detailed induction-machine electric drive as a WinchModels.AbstractWinchModel, so that KiteModels/KiteSimulators can use it in place of a simple winch.\n\nThe layering rule behind the whole package is:\n\nKiteModels owns the mechanical state, this package owns only the electrical state.\n\nThe machine speed is therefore imposed rather than integrated here:\n\nωm    = gear_ratio / drum_radius * v_reelout\nTload = drum_radius / gear_ratio * tether_force\n\nand the machine-side mechanics follow J*dωm/dt = Te + Tload - B*ωm.\n\nMain entry points:\n\nmake_electric_winch builds a DetailedIMWinch with one of the controllers :ideal, :foc_speed_f1 or :foc_speed_mtpa.\nstep_drive_from_kite! advances the drive once per KiteSimulators macro-step; this is the preferred integration path.\nWinchModels.calc_acceleration is the WinchModels interface method.\nlast_summary returns the most useful last-step values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.ElectricMachineWinch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/winch/#ElectricMachineWinch.AbstractIMDriveController","page":"Winch and interface","text":"AbstractIMDriveController\n\nAll machine-controller variants should subtype this and implement:\n\ndrive_step!(controller, plant; ωm_ref, ωm, Tload, Ts, plant_substeps)\n\nThe method must return a DriveStepOutput.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.AbstractIMDriveController"},{"category":"type","location":"api/winch/#ElectricMachineWinch.DetailedIMWinch","page":"Winch and interface","text":"DetailedIMWinch\n\nKiteSimulators/KiteModels-compatible winch model.\n\nIt subtypes WinchModels.AbstractWinchModel, so when KiteModels calls calc_acceleration(wm, ...), Julia dispatches to the method defined in this package.\n\nFields:\n\ndrum_radius: winch drum radius [m]\ngear_ratio: machine speed / drum speed [-]\nJ_eq: equivalent inertia referred to the machine shaft [kg m²]\nB_eq: equivalent viscous friction referred to the machine shaft [N m s/rad]\nTs: electrical/controller sample time [s]\nplant_substeps: RK4 substeps per controller sample\ncontroller: swappable controller object\nplant: induction-machine plant wrapper\nlast values: stored for logging/debugging\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.DetailedIMWinch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/winch/#ElectricMachineWinch.DriveStepOutput","page":"Winch and interface","text":"DriveStepOutput\n\nCommon output returned by every electric-drive controller implementation. This lets the winch wrapper stay controller-agnostic: FOC, ideal torque, future MPC, scalar V/f, etc. can all return the same information.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.DriveStepOutput"},{"category":"type","location":"api/winch/#ElectricMachineWinch.InductionMachinePlant","page":"Winch and interface","text":"InductionMachinePlant\n\nSmall mutable wrapper around the validated IMAWESbench plant parameters and state. This deliberately contains only the electrical machine state/parameters. The reel-out mechanics are owned by the KiteModels/WinchModels interface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.InductionMachinePlant"},{"category":"function","location":"api/winch/#ElectricMachineWinch.drive_step!","page":"Winch and interface","text":"drive_step!(controller, plant; ωm_ref, ωm, Tload, Ts, plant_substeps) -> DriveStepOutput\n\nAdvance one electric-drive controller sample and return the resulting DriveStepOutput.\n\nThis is the extension point of the package: every AbstractIMDriveController implements exactly this method, and because the return value is the same for all of them, DetailedIMWinch and the KiteModels interface stay controller-agnostic.\n\nArguments:\n\ncontroller: the controller object, whose discrete states are updated in place\nplant: InductionMachinePlant holding the electrical machine parameters and state; also updated in place by controllers that use it\n\nKeyword arguments:\n\nωm_ref: machine-side speed reference [rad/s], gear_ratio/drum_radius * v_ro_set\nωm: measured/imposed machine-side speed [rad/s], gear_ratio/drum_radius * v_reelout\nTload: machine-side load torque [Nm], drum_radius/gear_ratio * tether_force\nTs: controller sample time [s]\nplant_substeps: RK4 substeps of the electrical plant per controller sample\n\nSign convention, which every implementation must follow:\n\nJ*dωm/dt = Te + Tload - B*ωm\n\nA positive Tload pulls toward positive reel-out. During positive-speed generation expect ωm > 0, Tload > 0 and a negative electromagnetic torque Te.\n\nThe mechanical speed is imposed, never integrated: implementations synchronize plant.x.ωm with ωm and integrate the electrical states only, with rk4_step_electrical_only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.drive_step!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/winch/#ElectricMachineWinch.last_summary-Tuple{DetailedIMWinch}","page":"Winch and interface","text":"last_summary(wm)\n\nSmall NamedTuple with the most useful last-step values. Handy in REPL tests.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.last_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/winch/#ElectricMachineWinch.reset!","page":"Winch and interface","text":"reset!(wm::DetailedIMWinch)\nreset!(controller::AbstractIMDriveController)\n\nClear all stored last-step values and discrete controller/observer states, so that the same object can be reused for another run.\n\nreset!(wm) zeroes the logging fields and the n_acceleration_calls counter of the winch and then resets its controller. For controllers, the fallback reset!(::AbstractIMDriveController) = nothing covers stateless implementations; controllers with discrete states add their own method.\n\nNote that the electrical plant state is not reset by these methods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.reset!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/winch/#ElectricMachineWinch.step_drive_from_kite!-Tuple{DetailedIMWinch, Real, Real, Real}","page":"Winch and interface","text":"step_drive_from_kite!(wm, v_ro_set, v_ro_meas, tether_force; dt_outer)\n\nUpdate the electric drive/controller once from the KiteSimulators loop, but internally run the electrical/FOC model with its own smaller timestep wm.Ts.\n\nReturns the latest machine-side electromagnetic torque [Nm].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.step_drive_from_kite!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/winch/#WinchModels.calc_acceleration-Tuple{DetailedIMWinch, Any, Any}","page":"Winch and interface","text":"WinchModels.calc_acceleration(wm::DetailedIMWinch, v_reelout, tether_force; set_speed, set_torque, use_brake)\n\nReturn reel-out acceleration [m/s²] for KiteModels.\n\nInputs from KiteModels:\n\nv_reelout: current reel-out velocity [m/s]\ntether_force: tether tension / force at the winch [N]\nset_speed: reel-out speed reference [m/s], used by the autopilot path\nset_torque: optional machine-side torque command [Nm], useful for direct tests\n\nInternal sign convention:\n\nJ*dω = Te + Tload - B*ω\n\nwhere:\n\nTload = drum_radius / gear_ratio * tether_force\npositive Tload pulls toward positive reel-out speed\nduring positive-speed generation, Te is normally negative\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WinchModels.calc_acceleration"},{"category":"method","location":"api/winch/#ElectricMachineWinch.make_electric_winch-Tuple{}","page":"Winch and interface","text":"make_electric_winch(; controller=:ideal, ...)\n\nCreate a DetailedIMWinch with one of the available drive controllers:\n\ncontroller = :ideal Ideal electromagnetic-torque source for initial integration/debugging.\ncontroller = :foc_speed_f1 Existing IMAWESbench FOC speed controller with the F1 flux strategy.\ncontroller = :foc_speed_mtpa Discrete FOC speed controller with constrained MTPA current allocation.\n\nThe mechanical parameters J_eq and B_eq are used both by the winch model and by the MTPA speed controller. This keeps the speed-loop tuning and mechanical feedforward consistent with the plant seen by KiteModels.\n\nNotes\n\nuse_field_weakening and wm_base_fw currently apply only to :foc_speed_f1.\nThe present :foc_speed_mtpa controller does not yet implement field weakening.\nIs_max is interpreted as the maximum magnitude of the dq current vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.make_electric_winch"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#Extending-the-package-with-another-controller","page":"Extending","text":"Adding a controller requires no change to KiteControllers, KiteModels or KiteControllers — the whole integration is written against DriveStepOutput.\n\nCreate a new controller subtype:\n\nmutable struct MyController <: AbstractIMDriveController\n    # states and parameters\nend\n\nImplement drive_step!:\n\nfunction drive_step!(\n    controller::MyController,\n    plant::InductionMachinePlant;\n    ωm_ref,\n    ωm,\n    Tload,\n    Ts,\n    plant_substeps = 1,\n)\n    # observer/controller/plant update\n\n    return DriveStepOutput(\n        Te = ...,\n        Te_ref = ...,\n    )\nend\n\nIf the controller carries discrete states, also implement reset! for it. The fallback reset!(::AbstractIMDriveController) = nothing covers stateless controllers.\n\nThen:\n\ninclude and export the controller in src/ElectricMachineWinch.jl;\nadd a selector branch in src/constructors.jl;\nadd a standalone test, modeled on test/test_foc_speed_f1.jl;\nuse the new controller symbol in make_electric_winch.\n\nThe rest of the KiteControllers integration can remain unchanged.","title":"Extending the package with another controller"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#Things-to-get-right","page":"Extending","text":"The sign convention. J*dωm/dt = Te + Tload - B*ωm; see the home page. Anything else, and the speed will run away in a direction that looks like a controller bug.\nThe machine speed is imposed, not integrated. Use rk4_step_electrical_only rather than the IM_AWES_bench plant step, which would integrate a second, duplicate mechanical speed state.\nInclude order matters in src/ElectricMachineWinch.jl: controllers/foc_speed_mtpa.jl is included after constructors.jl.\nRevise does not pick up changes to struct definitions, module includes, or exports — restart Julia after those.","title":"Things to get right"},{"category":"section","location":"diagnostics/#Logging-and-diagnostics","page":"Diagnostics","text":"","title":"Logging and diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"diagnostics/#Controller-agnostic-values","page":"Diagnostics","text":"The latest common drive values are stored directly in DetailedIMWinch:\n\nwm.Te\nwm.Te_ref\n\nwm.isd_ref\nwm.isq_ref\nwm.isd\nwm.isq\n\nwm.vsd\nwm.vsq\nwm.vsα\nwm.vsβ\n\nwm.Pelec\nwm.Pmech\nwm.saturated\n\nA compact summary is available with:\n\nlast_summary(wm)\n\nwhich also reports n_acceleration_calls, the debug counter that reveals excessive DAE residual calls (see KiteModels integration).","title":"Controller-agnostic values"},{"category":"section","location":"diagnostics/#MTPA-specific-diagnostics","page":"Diagnostics","text":"For an MTPA controller, the full outer-loop output of the last step is retained:\n\nmtpa = wm.controller.last_outer\n\nUseful fields include:\n\nmtpa.isd_mtpa\nmtpa.isd_floor\nmtpa.isd_reserve\nmtpa.isd_desired\nmtpa.isd_ref\nmtpa.isq_ref\nmtpa.isq_max_disp\nmtpa.Te_ref_out\nmtpa.Te_current_limited\nmtpa.torque_current_limited\n\nThese signals make it possible to distinguish:\n\nthe pure MTPA solution;\nthe active minimum-flux or torque-reserve constraint;\nthe ramped d-axis reference;\ncurrent-circle limitation;\nrequested versus current-feasible torque.","title":"MTPA-specific diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"diagnostics/#Comparing-F1-and-MTPA-fairly","page":"Diagnostics","text":"When comparing:\n\n:foc_speed_f1\n\nand:\n\n:foc_speed_mtpa\n\nkeep the following identical unless the parameter itself is under study:\n\nkite and wind profile;\ninitial conditions;\ndrum radius and gear ratio;\nequivalent inertia and friction;\nspeed-loop tuning;\nsampling period and plant substeps;\ncurrent, voltage, and torque limits;\ncurrent-controller parameters;\nrotor-flux observer;\nload-feedforward configuration;\nKiteSimulators macro-step.\n\nUseful comparison metrics include:\n\nreel-out and reel-in speed RMSE;\nelectromagnetic-torque tracking;\nRMS dq-current magnitude;\nintegral of isd^2 + isq^2 as a current/copper-loss proxy;\nelectrical and mechanical energy;\ncurrent- and voltage-saturation time;\nMTPA flux-floor and torque-reserve activity;\ncycle completion and average generated power.\n\nBecause the current F1 controller supports field weakening while the current MTPA controller does not, a full-speed comparison is not yet strictly equivalent whenever the AWES cycle enters the field-weakening region. A first comparison should either remain below base speed or clearly report the voltage-limited intervals.","title":"Comparing F1 and MTPA fairly"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Getting-started-with-ElectricMachineWinch.jl","page":"Getting started","text":"This document explains what the bridge package does and how to start testing it.\n\n","title":"Getting started with ElectricMachineWinch.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#1.-Why-this-package-exists","page":"Getting started","text":"KiteControllers.jl should remain mostly untouched. The clean way to include a detailed electric machine model is to provide a new object that behaves like a WinchModels winch.\n\nKiteModels internally asks the winch model for acceleration:\n\ncalc_acceleration(wm, v_reelout, tether_force; set_speed = v_set)\n\nSo this bridge package defines:\n\nDetailedIMWinch <: WinchModels.AbstractWinchModel\n\nand implements:\n\nWinchModels.calc_acceleration(wm::DetailedIMWinch, ...)\n\nIt also provides step_drive_from_kite!, which is the preferred entry point for the detailed discrete controllers (see section 7).\n\n","title":"1. Why this package exists"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#2.-Requirements-and-folder-layout","page":"Getting started","text":"The package targets Julia 1.12 and depends on IM_AWES_bench and WinchModels.\n\nIM_AWES_bench is resolved from the git URL declared in [sources] in Project.toml, so no manual setup is needed for a plain checkout. If you also want to edit IM_AWES_bench locally, place the projects side by side:\n\nJuliaModels/\n├── ElectricMachineWinch.jl/\n├── IM_AWES_bench.jl/\n├── WinchModels.jl/\n└── KiteControllers.jl/\n\n","title":"2. Requirements and folder layout"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#3.-Configure-dependencies","page":"Getting started","text":"Default (git source) setup:\n\njulia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nTo switch IM_AWES_bench to the local sibling checkout ../IM_AWES_bench:\n\nbin/dev\n\nTo switch back to the git source:\n\nbin/free\n\nBoth scripts edit the [sources] section of Project.toml in place, so that file will show as modified afterwards.\n\nCheck that the package loads:\n\nusing ElectricMachineWinch\n\nbin/run_julia starts a REPL in this project with Revise loaded if available. Revise does not track changes to struct definitions, module includes, or exports, so restart Julia after changing those.\n\n","title":"3. Configure dependencies"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#4.-First-standalone-test:-ideal-torque-controller","page":"Getting started","text":"julia --project=test test/test_ideal_torque.jl\n\nor, in a running REPL:\n\ninclude(\"test/test_ideal_torque.jl\")\n\nThis test does not use KiteControllers. It checks only:\n\nv_set -> speed PI -> torque actuator -> winch acceleration -> v_reelout\n\nThe important thing is not the exact numbers, but that the script runs without errors and that speed, torque, and acceleration stay finite.\n\nIf this fails, debug the bridge package before connecting KiteControllers.\n\n","title":"4. First standalone test: ideal torque controller"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#5.-Second-standalone-test:-full-FOC-speed-controller","page":"Getting started","text":"julia --project=test test/test_foc_speed_f1.jl\n\nThis checks:\n\nv_set\n  -> rotor-flux observer\n  -> FOC outer speed/flux controller\n  -> current controller\n  -> inverse Park -> alpha-beta voltage\n  -> electrical-only IM RK4 plant\n  -> electromagnetic torque\n  -> winch acceleration\n\nThis is the first test involving the detailed IM_AWES_bench model.\n\nRun the whole suite with:\n\njulia --project=test test/runtests.jl\n\nIf the speed goes the wrong way, check sign conventions first. The convention is:\n\nJ*dωm/dt = Te + Tload - B*ωm\n\nwhere:\n\nTload = drum_radius / gear_ratio * tether_force\nωm    = gear_ratio / drum_radius * v_reelout\n\nDuring positive-speed generation, you usually expect:\n\nv_reelout > 0\nTload > 0\nTe < 0\n\n","title":"5. Second standalone test: full FOC speed controller"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#6.-Constructing-a-winch","page":"Getting started","text":"make_electric_winch selects the controller and builds a coherent set of IM_AWES_bench parameter objects:\n\nwm = make_electric_winch(\n    controller = :ideal,\n    drum_radius = 0.5,\n    gear_ratio = 10.0,\n    J_eq = 0.3685,\n    B_eq = 0.01298,\n    Ts = 1e-3,\n    Te_max = 124.0,\n)\n\nAvailable controllers are :ideal, :foc_speed_f1, and :foc_speed_mtpa. See Controllers for the full parameter sets of the two FOC variants.\n\n","title":"6. Constructing a winch"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#7.-Connecting-to-KiteControllers-autopilot","page":"Getting started","text":"In your KiteControllers.jl project, add the bridge package to the environment:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.activate(\".\")\nPkg.develop(path=\"../ElectricMachineWinch.jl\")\nPkg.instantiate()\n\nThen copy the original autopilot example:\n\nexamples/autopilot.jl -> examples/autopilot_im_winch.jl\n\nand add near the top:\n\nusing ElectricMachineWinch\n\nImmediately after app.kps4 = KPS4(app.kcu), insert the winch. Start with the simple ideal-torque controller:\n\napp.kps4.wm = make_electric_winch(\n    controller = :ideal,\n    drum_radius = 0.5,\n    gear_ratio = 10.0,\n    J_eq = 0.3685,\n    B_eq = 0.01298,\n    Ts = app.dt,\n    Te_max = 124.0,\n)\n\nOnly after that works, switch to the FOC model:\n\napp.kps4.wm = make_electric_winch(\n    controller = :foc_speed_f1,\n    drum_radius = 0.2,\n    gear_ratio = 4.26,\n    J_eq = 0.204,\n    B_eq = 0.0804,\n    Ts = 1e-4,\n    plant_substeps = 1,\n    Vs_max = 310.0,\n    Is_max = 40.0 * sqrt(2),\n    Te_max = 124.0,\n    speed_ts_wm = 1.0,\n    use_load_feedforward = true,\n    use_field_weakening = true,\n    wm_base_fw = 120.0,\n)","title":"7. Connecting to KiteControllers autopilot"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Stepping-the-drive","page":"Getting started","text":"Update the electric drive exactly once per KiteControllers macro-step, then hand the resulting torque to the mechanical update:\n\nTe_cmd = ElectricMachineWinch.step_drive_from_kite!(\n    app.kps4.wm,\n    v_ro_set,\n    v_ro_meas,\n    F_tether;\n    dt_outer = app.dt,\n)\n\nKiteModels.next_step!(app.kps4, integrator; set_torque = Te_cmd, dt = app.dt)\n\nstep_drive_from_kite! runs enough electrical/controller substeps to cover the macro-step, holding the kite-side variables constant during it. Choose dt_outer so that dt_outer / wm.Ts is an integer (or very close to one); otherwise the effective sample time drifts away from the Ts stored in the observer and controller parameter objects.\n\nThis pattern is required for detailed stateful controllers because a DAE solver may evaluate the mechanical residual more than once per macro-step. Advancing the discrete observer/controller states inside every residual evaluation would produce a nonphysical number of controller updates. wm.n_acceleration_calls counts the calls so this is easy to detect.\n\nThe direct WinchModels.calc_acceleration(...; set_speed = ...) path, which steps the drive inside the call, remains available for simple integration tests and compatibility.","title":"Stepping the drive"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Working-examples","page":"Getting started","text":"examples/autopilot_im_winch_FOC_F1.jl — a complete autopilot script using the F1 controller, including debug logging and CSV export of the drive signals.\nexamples/autopilot_im_winch_patch.jl — not runnable on its own; it is the minimal patch to apply to a fresh copy of the upstream autopilot example.\n\nSee KiteModels integration for the full picture of the two integration paths.\n\n","title":"Working examples"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#8.-What-each-source-file-does","page":"Getting started","text":"","title":"8. What each source file does"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#src/types.jl","page":"Getting started","text":"Defines the core objects and thereby the architecture:\n\nDriveStepOutput — the common return value of every controller\nInductionMachinePlant — electrical plant parameters and state\nAbstractIMDriveController — the controller interface\nDetailedIMWinch — the WinchModels-compatible winch, plus reset! and last_summary","title":"src/types.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#src/plant_steps.jl","page":"Getting started","text":"The electrical-only induction-machine RK4 step. It uses the same alpha-beta electrical equations as the benchmark, but does not let the IM plant integrate its own mechanical speed — KiteModels already owns the reel-out speed state. Also contains im_torque, inverse_park_voltage, and phase_power_alpha_beta.","title":"src/plant_steps.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#src/controllers/ideal_torque.jl","page":"Getting started","text":"Speed PI plus a first-order torque actuator with rate and magnitude limits. No electrical plant. Used to debug the KiteControllers coupling before adding the machine model.","title":"src/controllers/ideal_torque.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#src/controllers/foc_speed_f1.jl","page":"Getting started","text":"Adapter around the validated FOC speed/flux F1 blocks from IM_AWES_bench. Supports field weakening.","title":"src/controllers/foc_speed_f1.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#src/controllers/foc_speed_mtpa.jl","page":"Getting started","text":"Adapter around the constrained-MTPA outer speed controller from IM_AWES_bench. The full outer-loop output is retained in wm.controller.last_outer for diagnostics (isd_mtpa, isd_floor, isd_reserve, Te_current_limited, ...).","title":"src/controllers/foc_speed_mtpa.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#src/winch_interface.jl","page":"Getting started","text":"WinchModels.calc_acceleration for DetailedIMWinch, and step_drive_from_kite!.","title":"src/winch_interface.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#src/constructors.jl","page":"Getting started","text":"make_electric_winch(...), which selects the controller and validates the configuration.\n\n","title":"src/constructors.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#9.-Logging-and-diagnostics","page":"Getting started","text":"The latest controller-agnostic values are stored on the winch itself:\n\nwm.Te, wm.Te_ref\nwm.isd_ref, wm.isq_ref, wm.isd, wm.isq\nwm.vsd, wm.vsq, wm.vsα, wm.vsβ\nwm.Pelec, wm.Pmech, wm.saturated\n\nA compact NamedTuple is available with:\n\nlast_summary(wm)\n\n","title":"9. Logging and diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#10.-Adding-another-controller","page":"Getting started","text":"Do not modify KiteControllers. Add a new controller type:\n\nmutable struct MyNewController <: AbstractIMDriveController\n    # states and parameters\nend\n\nImplement:\n\nfunction drive_step!(\n    c::MyNewController,\n    plant::InductionMachinePlant;\n    ωm_ref,\n    ωm,\n    Tload,\n    Ts,\n    plant_substeps = 1,\n)\n    # controller + plant logic\n    return DriveStepOutput(Te = ..., Te_ref = ...)\nend\n\nThen include and export it in src/ElectricMachineWinch.jl, add a selector branch in src/constructors.jl, and add a test file modeled on test/test_foc_speed_f1.jl. The rest of the KiteControllers integration stays unchanged.\n\n","title":"10. Adding another controller"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#11.-Current-limitations","page":"Getting started","text":"The MTPA controller does not implement field weakening yet, so use_field_weakening and wm_base_fw apply only to :foc_speed_f1. A full-speed comparison of the two controllers is not strictly equivalent once the cycle enters the field-weakening region.\nThere is no test/test_foc_speed_mtpa.jl yet; it can be added following test/test_foc_speed_f1.jl with controller = :foc_speed_mtpa.\nUsing calc_acceleration(...; set_speed = ...) inside a DAE solve advances the discrete controller states once per residual evaluation. Prefer step_drive_from_kite! for research runs.","title":"11. Current limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/controllers/#Drive-controllers","page":"Controllers","text":"Every controller implements the drive_step! contract documented under Core types.","title":"Drive controllers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/controllers/#Ideal-torque-controller","page":"Controllers","text":"","title":"Ideal torque controller"},{"category":"section","location":"api/controllers/#FOC-speed-controller,-F1-flux-strategy","page":"Controllers","text":"","title":"FOC speed controller, F1 flux strategy"},{"category":"section","location":"api/controllers/#FOC-speed-controller,-constrained-MTPA","page":"Controllers","text":"","title":"FOC speed controller, constrained MTPA"},{"category":"type","location":"api/controllers/#ElectricMachineWinch.IdealTorqueController","page":"Controllers","text":"IdealTorqueController\n\nMinimal controller for debugging the KiteSimulators integration before adding full IM electrical dynamics.\n\nMechanical convention:\n\nJ*dω = Te + Tload - B*ω\n\nA positive tether/load torque Tload pulls the winch in the positive reel-out speed direction. During generation/braking at positive reel-out speed, Te is usually negative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.IdealTorqueController"},{"category":"type","location":"api/controllers/#ElectricMachineWinch.FOCSpeedF1Controller","page":"Controllers","text":"FOCSpeedF1Controller\n\nController object holding the observer, outer speed/flux F1 loop, and current controller states and parameters from IM_AWES_bench.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.FOCSpeedF1Controller"},{"category":"method","location":"api/controllers/#ElectricMachineWinch.make_default_foc_speed_controller-Tuple{}","page":"Controllers","text":"make_default_foc_speed_controller(; Ts, Vs_max, Is_max, ...)\n\nConvenience constructor that creates a coherent FOC speed controller using the same default machine constants as your uploaded benchmark package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.make_default_foc_speed_controller"},{"category":"type","location":"api/controllers/#ElectricMachineWinch.FOCSpeedMTPAController","page":"Controllers","text":"FOCSpeedMTPAController\n\nElectricMachineWinch adapter for the discrete constrained-MTPA speed controller from IM_AWES_bench.\n\nThe object owns the states and parameters of:\n\nthe discrete rotor-flux observer;\nthe outer speed/MTPA controller;\nthe inner discrete current controller.\n\nlast_outer retains the complete OuterSpeedFluxMTPAOutput from the most recent controller step so that the KiteSimulators example can log MTPA-specific diagnostics such as isd_mtpa, isd_floor, isd_reserve, Te_current_limited, and torque_current_limited.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.FOCSpeedMTPAController"},{"category":"method","location":"api/controllers/#ElectricMachineWinch.FOCSpeedMTPAController-Tuple{}","page":"Controllers","text":"FOCSpeedMTPAController(; obs_p, outer_p, current_p)\n\nConstruct an MTPA speed-controller adapter from explicit observer, outer-loop, and current-controller parameter structures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.FOCSpeedMTPAController"},{"category":"method","location":"api/controllers/#ElectricMachineWinch.drive_step!-Tuple{FOCSpeedMTPAController, InductionMachinePlant}","page":"Controllers","text":"drive_step!(\n    controller::FOCSpeedMTPAController,\n    plant::InductionMachinePlant;\n    ωm_ref,\n    ωm,\n    Tload,\n    Ts,\n    plant_substeps = 1,\n)\n\nAdvance the complete electrical drive by one controller sample.\n\nArguments\n\nωm_ref: machine mechanical-speed reference [rad/s].\nωm: machine mechanical speed imposed by KiteModels [rad/s].\nTload: equivalent machine-side tether/load torque [N*m].\nTs: controller/electrical step [s].\nplant_substeps: electrical RK4 substeps inside Ts.\n\nReturns\n\nA common DriveStepOutput used by DetailedIMWinch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.drive_step!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/controllers/#ElectricMachineWinch.make_default_foc_speed_mtpa_controller-Tuple{}","page":"Controllers","text":"make_default_foc_speed_mtpa_controller(; kwargs...)\n\nCreate a coherent constrained-MTPA FOC speed controller for use inside DetailedIMWinch.\n\nThe parameters J and B must describe the same equivalent machine-side mechanics as DetailedIMWinch.J_eq and DetailedIMWinch.B_eq. The make_electric_winch constructor should therefore pass its J_eq and B_eq values into this function.\n\nThe MTPA torque model is\n\nTe = Kt_isd * isd * isq\n\nwhere\n\nKt_isd = 1.5 * pole_pairs * Lm^2 / Lrr.\n\nIs_max is interpreted as the maximum magnitude of the dq current vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.make_default_foc_speed_mtpa_controller"},{"category":"method","location":"api/controllers/#ElectricMachineWinch.reset!-Tuple{FOCSpeedMTPAController}","page":"Controllers","text":"reset!(controller::FOCSpeedMTPAController)\n\nReset all discrete controller/observer states and clear the stored MTPA diagnostics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.reset!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/plant/#Electrical-plant","page":"Electrical plant","text":"The electrical-only αβ induction-machine step. It mirrors the validated IM_AWES_bench machine equations, but does not integrate the mechanical speed: KiteModels already owns the reel-out speed state, so the machine speed is imposed here.","title":"Electrical plant"},{"category":"method","location":"api/plant/#ElectricMachineWinch.im_torque-Tuple{IM_AWES_bench.IMPlantState, IM_AWES_bench.IMPlantParams}","page":"Electrical plant","text":"im_torque(x, p)\n\nElectromagnetic torque of the IM model, using the same expression as IMAWESbench. Reimplemented here to avoid relying on non-exported internals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.im_torque"},{"category":"method","location":"api/plant/#ElectricMachineWinch.inverse_park_voltage-Tuple{Float64, Float64, Float64}","page":"Electrical plant","text":"inverse_park_voltage(vsd, vsq, theta_e)\n\nInverse Park transformation of the stator voltage: rotate the dq voltage (vsd, vsq) by the electrical field angle theta_e [rad] into the stationary αβ frame. Returns the tuple (vsα, vsβ) fed to rk4_step_electrical_only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.inverse_park_voltage"},{"category":"method","location":"api/plant/#ElectricMachineWinch.phase_power_alpha_beta-NTuple{4, Float64}","page":"Electrical plant","text":"phase_power_alpha_beta(vsα, vsβ, isα, isβ)\n\nInstantaneous three-phase electrical power [W] of the stator, computed by expanding the αβ voltages and currents back into phase quantities and summing va*ia + vb*ib + vc*ic. Reported as Pelec in DriveStepOutput.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.phase_power_alpha_beta"},{"category":"method","location":"api/plant/#ElectricMachineWinch.rk4_step_electrical_only-Tuple{IM_AWES_bench.IMPlantState, IM_AWES_bench.IMPlantParams, Float64}","page":"Electrical plant","text":"rk4_step_electrical_only(x, p, h; vsα, vsβ, imposed_ωm)\n\nIntegrate only the electrical αβ IM dynamics and mechanical angle for one RK4 substep. Mechanical speed is imposed from KiteModels reel-out speed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.rk4_step_electrical_only"},{"category":"section","location":"#ElectricMachineWinch.jl","page":"Home","text":"ElectricMachineWinch.jl is a bridge package for testing detailed induction-machine electric drives as winch models in the KiteControllers/KiteModels ecosystem. It provides a winch type compatible with WinchModels,\n\nDetailedIMWinch <: WinchModels.AbstractWinchModel\n\nand connects it to interchangeable electric-drive controllers built from the discrete induction-machine blocks of IM_AWES_bench. The package contains almost no control math of its own — the controllers here are thin adapters around IM_AWES_bench blocks.","title":"ElectricMachineWinch.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-layering-rule","page":"Home","text":"The whole reason this package exists is a strict split of ownership:\n\nKiteModels owns the mechanical state, this package owns only the electrical state.\n\nKiteModels owns reel-out speed, tether mechanics, and the equivalent winch mechanical state.\nElectricMachineWinch owns the electric-drive controller, observer, and induction-machine electrical states.\nIM_AWES_bench provides the validated controller, observer, and machine-model building blocks.\n\nThe machine speed is therefore imposed from the measured reel-out speed and never integrated here, and the tether force is converted into an equivalent machine-side load torque:\n\nωm    = gear_ratio / drum_radius * v_reelout\nTload = drum_radius / gear_ratio * tether_force\n\nOnly the electrical machine states are integrated internally, by rk4_step_electrical_only. This avoids introducing a second mechanical-speed state in parallel with the state already owned by KiteModels.","title":"The layering rule"},{"category":"section","location":"#Sign-convention","page":"Home","text":"The machine-side mechanical model uses:\n\nJ * dωm/dt = Te + Tload - B * ωm\n\nwith:\n\nTload = drum_radius / gear_ratio * tether_force\nωm    = gear_ratio / drum_radius * v_reelout\n\nFor positive-speed generation, the typical signs are:\n\nv_reelout > 0\nTload > 0\nTe < 0\n\nA positive load torque pulls the winch toward positive reel-out speed. The load-feedforward sign must be consistent with this convention; the MTPA constructor exposes load_ff_sign, with the current default:\n\nload_ff_sign = -1.0\n\nWhen a test or a run shows the speed going the wrong way, check this convention before suspecting the controller.","title":"Sign convention"},{"category":"section","location":"#Architecture","page":"Home","text":"KiteControllers autopilot\n        │\n        │ reel-out speed reference\n        ▼\nKiteModels / KPS4\n        │\n        │ v_ro_set, v_ro_meas, tether_force\n        ▼\nElectricMachineWinch.DetailedIMWinch\n        │\n        ├── IdealTorqueController\n        │\n        ├── FOCSpeedF1Controller\n        │   └── IM_AWES_bench F1 outer speed/flux loop\n        │\n        └── FOCSpeedMTPAController\n            └── IM_AWES_bench constrained-MTPA outer speed loop\n        │\n        ▼\nelectromagnetic torque\n        │\n        ▼\nKiteModels mechanical update\n\nAll drive controllers subtype AbstractIMDriveController and implement drive_step!, which returns the same DriveStepOutput for every controller. This keeps DetailedIMWinch and the KiteModels interface independent of the selected controller.","title":"Architecture"},{"category":"section","location":"#Controllers","page":"Home","text":"Symbol Type When to use it\n:ideal IdealTorqueController First integration test. A speed PI plus a first-order torque actuator, with no electrical plant at all — use it to debug the KiteModels coupling before adding observer, current controller and machine model.\n:foc_speed_f1 FOCSpeedF1Controller The reference detailed drive. Full discrete FOC with the F1 flux strategy, and the only controller that currently supports field weakening.\n:foc_speed_mtpa FOCSpeedMTPAController Energy-optimal current allocation with practical constraints (flux floor, torque reserve, current circle). No field weakening yet.\n\nSee Controllers for the details and the full parameter sets.","title":"Controllers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quickstart","page":"Home","text":"The package targets Julia 1.12 and depends on IM_AWES_bench and WinchModels. IM_AWES_bench is unregistered and is resolved from the git URL declared in [sources] in Project.toml, so a plain checkout needs no manual setup:\n\njulia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nStart a REPL in the package environment (Revise is loaded if available) with:\n\nbin/run_julia\n\nBuild a winch and inspect its last-step values:\n\nusing ElectricMachineWinch\n\nwm = make_electric_winch(\n    controller = :ideal,\n    drum_radius = 0.5,\n    gear_ratio = 10.0,\n    J_eq = 0.3685,\n    B_eq = 0.01298,\n    Ts = 1e-3,\n    Te_max = 124.0,\n)\n\nlast_summary(wm)\n\nRun the test suite:\n\njulia --project=test test/runtests.jl","title":"Quickstart"},{"category":"section","location":"#API","page":"Home","text":"Pages = [\"api/winch.md\", \"api/controllers.md\", \"api/plant.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"controllers/#Controllers","page":"Controllers","text":"Three drive controllers are available today. All of them subtype AbstractIMDriveController, implement drive_step!, and return a DriveStepOutput, so switching between them changes nothing on the KiteModels side.","title":"Controllers"},{"category":"section","location":"controllers/#IdealTorqueController","page":"Controllers","text":"A simplified torque actuator with a speed PI.\n\nUse it first to verify:\n\nautopilot speed reference\n    -> winch controller\n    -> electromagnetic torque\n    -> KiteModels mechanics\n\nwithout simultaneously debugging the induction-machine observer, current controller, and electrical plant.\n\nSelect it with:\n\ncontroller = :ideal","title":"IdealTorqueController"},{"category":"section","location":"controllers/#FOCSpeedF1Controller","page":"Controllers","text":"Wrapper around the discrete FOC speed-control path from IM_AWES_bench:\n\nrotor_flux_observer_step!\n    -> outer_speed_flux_f1_step!\n    -> current_controller_step!\n    -> inverse Park transformation\n    -> electrical-only alpha-beta IM RK4 step\n    -> electromagnetic torque\n\nThe F1 controller uses its configured flux-producing-current strategy and supports the field-weakening options exposed by make_electric_winch:\n\nuse_field_weakening\nwm_base_fw\n\nSelect it with:\n\ncontroller = :foc_speed_f1","title":"FOCSpeedF1Controller"},{"category":"section","location":"controllers/#FOCSpeedMTPAController","page":"Controllers","text":"Wrapper around the constrained-MTPA discrete speed controller from IM_AWES_bench:\n\nrotor_flux_observer_step!\n    -> outer_speed_flux_mtpa_step!\n    -> current_controller_step!\n    -> inverse Park transformation\n    -> electrical-only alpha-beta IM RK4 step\n    -> electromagnetic torque\n\nThe MTPA torque-current relationship is:\n\nTe = Kt_isd * isd * isq\n\nKt_isd = 1.5 * pole_pairs * Lm^2 / Lrr\n\nThe pure MTPA solution is supplemented by practical constraints:\n\nminimum d-axis current;\nrotor-flux floor;\ntorque reserve;\nd-axis current slew-rate limit;\ntotal dq-current circle;\nelectromagnetic-torque limit.\n\nSelect it with:\n\ncontroller = :foc_speed_mtpa\n\nThe full outer-controller output is retained in:\n\nwm.controller.last_outer\n\nwhich exposes MTPA-specific diagnostics such as:\n\nisd_mtpa\nisd_floor\nisd_reserve\nisd_desired\nisq_max_disp\nTe_current_limited\ntorque_current_limited\n\nSee Logging and diagnostics for the full list.","title":"FOCSpeedMTPAController"},{"category":"section","location":"controllers/#Current-MTPA-limitation","page":"Controllers","text":"The present MTPA controller does not yet include field weakening.\n\nTherefore:\n\nuse_field_weakening\nwm_base_fw\n\ncurrently apply only to :foc_speed_f1.\n\nFor full-speed AWES cycles, first verify whether the MTPA case reaches the inverter voltage limit during high-speed reel-in. A future combined MTPA/field-weakening controller should coordinate the energy-optimal current allocation below base speed with the voltage-feasibility constraint above base speed.","title":"Current MTPA limitation"},{"category":"section","location":"controllers/#Constructing-a-winch","page":"Controllers","text":"make_electric_winch selects the controller and builds a coherent set of IM_AWES_bench parameter objects.","title":"Constructing a winch"},{"category":"section","location":"controllers/#Ideal-controller","page":"Controllers","text":"using ElectricMachineWinch\n\nwm = make_electric_winch(\n    controller = :ideal,\n    drum_radius = 0.5,\n    gear_ratio = 10.0,\n    J_eq = 0.3685,\n    B_eq = 0.01298,\n    Ts = 1e-3,\n    Te_max = 124.0,\n)","title":"Ideal controller"},{"category":"section","location":"controllers/#F1-controller","page":"Controllers","text":"wm = make_electric_winch(\n    controller = :foc_speed_f1,\n\n    drum_radius = 0.2,\n    gear_ratio = 4.26,\n    J_eq = 0.204,\n    B_eq = 0.0804,\n\n    Ts = 100e-6,\n    plant_substeps = 1,\n\n    Vs_max = 310.0,\n    Is_max = 40.0 * sqrt(2.0),\n    Te_max = 124.0,\n\n    speed_ts_wm = 1.0,\n    speed_tau_f_wm = 10e-3,\n    speed_ts_dist_wm = 3.0,\n    wm_dot_max = 100.0,\n\n    use_load_feedforward = true,\n\n    use_field_weakening = true,\n    wm_base_fw = 120.0,\n)","title":"F1 controller"},{"category":"section","location":"controllers/#Constrained-MTPA-controller","page":"Controllers","text":"wm = make_electric_winch(\n    controller = :foc_speed_mtpa,\n\n    drum_radius = 0.2,\n    gear_ratio = 4.26,\n    J_eq = 0.204,\n    B_eq = 0.0804,\n\n    Ts = 100e-6,\n    plant_substeps = 1,\n\n    Vs_max = 310.0,\n    Is_max = 40.0 * sqrt(2.0),\n    Te_max = 124.0,\n\n    speed_ts_wm = 1.0,\n    speed_tau_f_wm = 10e-3,\n    speed_ts_dist_wm = 3.0,\n    wm_dot_max = 100.0,\n\n    use_load_feedforward = true,\n    load_ff_sign = -1.0,\n\n    pole_pairs = 2.0,\n    Lm = 40.84e-3,\n    Lrr = 45.12e-3,\n\n    isd_nom = 23.04579328,\n    isd_min = 5.0,\n    id_dot_max = 600.0,\n    lambda_rd_floor = 0.35,\n    Te_reserve = 45.0,\n)","title":"Constrained-MTPA controller"},{"category":"section","location":"controllers/#Parameter-notes","page":"Controllers","text":"Is_max is interpreted as the maximum magnitude of the dq current vector, not as an RMS phase rating. Use the actual peak-vector limit of the modeled machine and converter. The expression 40.0 * sqrt(2.0) is appropriate only when 40 A is an RMS phase-current rating that is intentionally converted to a peak value.\n\nFor the MTPA controller, J_eq and B_eq are passed into both:\n\nDetailedIMWinch;\nthe MTPA outer speed controller.\n\nThis keeps the speed-loop tuning and the inertia/friction feedforward consistent with the mechanical plant seen by KiteModels.\n\nCustom F1 parameter objects (obs_p, outer_f1_p, current_p) are all-or-nothing, and each must carry a Ts matching the winch Ts. The validation is deliberately strict, to prevent mixing a new-machine observer with the default small-machine loops.","title":"Parameter notes"}]
}
